Description: antinomie: read inputs that were not resident at entry again at exit
Author: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Origin: project
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-10-22

The recorder copies user memory without faulting, so an input on a page the program has mapped but not touched yet (a forked child's literals, static glibc's start-up readlinkat of /proc/self/exe, an untouched file mapping) is recorded NOT_RESIDENT at entry, although the call itself faults the page in.  Such an input is now remembered in the task's context (at most eight per call, nothing allocated) and read again at the end of the call's Exit, still without faulting. A string is measured again; a buffer is published only if every page reads without a fault. It goes into the Exit event under the Entry's key, as an input, with bit 3 (RECAPTURED) set in the FIELD_BEGIN flags word; a field that still cannot be read is not published, and an Exit field of the same key (the call's own output) drops the second read. The exec vectors keep the point where their entry walk stopped and resume from it. A vector pointer on a non-resident page is now NOT_RESIDENT like any other field instead of COPY_FAULT.  A successful exec replaced the caller's memory, so nothing is read from it: the path is the exec's own copy (bprm->filename, the caller's part of an execveat path relative to a descriptor), kept by a new hook at the commit only when the Entry could not read it, and the argument and environment strings from the resume point on are read from the new image's stack, which no user code has run on yet. Those fields carry bit 4 (KERNEL_COPY) too. An interpreter exec rewrote argv, so only its environment is recovered.  The FIELD_BEGIN flags word is not read by earlier hosts, so the change is additive and the contract revision stays 17.

